/-!
# EqualRung_Dist — the distance certificate for the 13-move witness

READ-ONLY scratch card.  Zero `sorry`, zero `Classical.choice`, zero
`native_decide`; battery at the tail.

## Why this shape (and not per-round `rfl`, and not one big decide)

* the per-round plan (`reachStep B(k−1) = Bk` by `rfl`) evaluates one whole
  round of `(ball).flatMap succsOf` — thousands of live `State` terms, each
  with function fields — which is what exhausted memory the first time;
* the entries' keys are STORED VALUES and the query's key is computed once —
  a lookup recomputing `skey` per entry per recursion is gigabytes of
  transients (the second failure);
* the certificate check is CHUNKED (40 decides of ~10 states each, assembled
  by rewriting whose kernel check is trivial) — one decide over all 392
  states dies in the kernel's lazy re-evaluation, which has no heartbeat
  budget and retains the whole spine (the third failure);
* there is only ONE table (`distTab` = the concatenation of the chunks) — an
  equality theorem between two spellings of the table forces the kernel to
  compare 392 literal STATES (each with function fields), the fourth failure.

The content: `distTab` is the whole reversible component of the witness
board — 392 `(state, (full skey, distance-to-v))` entries, generated by
`Temp/EqualRung_dist_gen.py` (BFS from `v` over the exact model; Python also
re-checked every certificate entry).  `D st` is the table lookup (`999` =
outside).  `certOK st` checks, at ONE state in isolation, that every
reversible candidate move either does not fire, or lands inside the table
with the distance dropping by at most one.  `play_bound` is the induction: a
witness play from a tabled state stays tabled (the closedness conjunct) and
`D` drops by at most one per letter, so `D u = 13` and `D v = 0` force length
≥ 13.

Sanity on the Python side: the 13-letter play replays to exactly the dumped
`v`; the component has 392 states; `D u = 13`.
-/

